              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A future-tense verb — the other half of \"see you again.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "મ (ma) + ળી (ḷī, with the retroflex ળ ḷ) + શું (shũ) becomes મળીશું (maḷīshũ)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "મળીશું (maḷīshũ, \"we will meet\") is the future of મળવું (maḷvũ, \"to meet\"), from Sanskrit મિલ્ (mil-, \"to join, unite\") — the same root as Hindi milnā and Punjabi milṇā. Note the retroflex ળ (ḷ): Gujarati keeps this extra letter — like Marathi and the Dravidian south, and unlike Hindi. The ending -શું (-shũ) is the \"we will\" future."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the future is an ending",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Gujarati marks the future with an ending on the stem: maḷ- (\"meet\") + -īshũ (\"we will\") becomes maḷīshũ. No helper-word like English \"will.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
