    {
      "id": "GU-C04-kaale",
      "sequence": 230,
      "title": "કાલે મળીશું (kāle maḷīshũ) — \"see you tomorrow\"",
      "headword": "કાલે મળીશું",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "see you tomorrow (and the કાલ puzzle)",
      "script": "gujarati",
      "modality": "sight",
      "derivedModality": "sight",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "script-block"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:d762ef2a619956f7",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "sight",
        "needs": [
          "your eyes, for letter shapes on the page"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [
          "The letters in this word"
        ],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, for letter shapes on the page. You can listen to everything else now — leave the section called The letters in this word until you have stopped, and I will say so again when we reach it."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A goodbye for tomorrow — built on a word that means both tomorrow and yesterday."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "The letters in this word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "કા (kā) + લે (le) becomes કાલે (kāle, \"on [the] kāl\"). The base is કાલ (kāl)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "કાલ (kāl) means both \"tomorrow\" and \"yesterday\" — the day on either side of today — from Sanskrit કાલ (kāla, \"time\"). (It is the very same kāla behind Hindi's kal, Bengali's kāl, and Punjabi's akāl \"timeless.\") The verb's tense tells which: kāle maḷīshũ (future becomes \"tomorrow we'll meet\"), kāle maḷyā (past becomes \"we met yesterday\"). So કાલે મળીશું = \"tomorrow we'll meet\" = \"see you tomorrow.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"kāle maḷīshũ\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"kāle maḷīshũ\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the puzzle — kāl means both tomorrow and yesterday",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the puzzle — *kāl* means both **tomorrow** and **yesterday**]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the root and its cousins (kāla \"time\"; Hindi kal, Punjabi akāl)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the root and its cousins (*kāla* \"time\"; Hindi *kal*, Punjabi *akāl*)]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What two opposite days does kāl mean, and what tells them apart? (Both tomorrow and yesterday; the verb's tense.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
